# Passing Data via URL Parameters Instead of Forms

You can absolutely link from a list view to a detail view while populating your form fields without creating a separate HTML form tag for every single item in the list. This is a standard pattern known as passing query parameters or using URL fragments to carry state between requests. In your specific scenario, you want the "Street View" page to contain links that automatically load the "House View" with the specific `house_number` pre-populated when clicked.

The most straightforward approach involves modifying your link tags in the Street View template. Instead of linking to a static path like `/house-view`, you append the house number as a query parameter, for example, `/house-view?house=12`. When the user clicks this link, your Laravel application (or any PHP framework) intercepts the request. You then read the `house` value from the `$_GET` superglobal array and pass it to your controller logic to fetch the resident data.

```php
// In your Street View Blade template or view
@foreach($houses as $house)
    <a href="{{ route('house.view', ['house' => $house->number]) }}">
        {{ $house->address }}
    </a>
@endforeach
```

On the backend, your controller method needs to handle this parameter. You can define a route that accepts an optional argument or strictly requires it depending on your design preference. If you choose to require it, you ensure that the URL always explicitly states which house is being viewed, which improves SEO and clarity.

```php
// routes/web.php
Route::get('/house-view/{house}', [HouseController::class, 'show']);
```

In the controller, you extract the parameter and query your database:

```php
// app/Http/Controllers/HouseController.php
public function show($house)
{
    $residents = Resident::where('house_number', $house)->get();

    // Pass data to the view
    return view('house-view', compact('residents'));
}
```

A critical consideration here is how your form on the House View page handles this data. Since you are avoiding a POST form submission for navigation, the fields in your House View template should ideally be pre-filled using the data retrieved in the controller. This creates a seamless user experience where clicking a link feels instantaneous and informative, rather than requiring a submit action to display content.

```php
// house-view.blade.php
<form method="POST" action="/house-view">
    @csrf

    <input type="text" name="house" value="{{ old('house', $house) }}" required>

    <!-- Display residents associated with the loaded house -->
    <ul>
        @foreach($residents as $resident)
            <li>{{ $resident->name }}</li>
        @endforeach
    </ul>

    <button type="submit">Update</button>
</form>
```

However, relying solely on GET parameters for data entry has limitations. The HTTP GET method is intended for retrieving information and should generally be idempotent. If your House View form is designed to perform a write operation (like adding a new resident or updating an existing record), sending the `house` number via GET is fine for navigation, but the actual data mutation must still happen via a POST request.

The trade-off with this approach is that the house number becomes visible in the URL bar. While this is standard practice and often preferred by search engines, it means you cannot hide sensitive internal IDs from users unless you use a different strategy like session storage or hidden inputs populated via JavaScript after an initial AJAX load. Additionally, if your application supports very deep URLs with many parameters, browser history management might become cumbersome if the user navigates back and forth frequently without clearing the query string.

If you prefer to keep the URL clean (e.g., `/house-view/12` instead of `/house-view?house=12`) while still avoiding a form submission for navigation, you can utilize route binding or named routes that accept the parameter directly as part of the path. This is often cleaner and avoids the clutter of query strings for primary identifiers.

For more complex scenarios where you need to manage state without exposing it in the URL, Laravel's session middleware can store the selected house number temporarily. You could set a session variable upon clicking a link on the Street View page and then read that session variable in the House View form. This decouples the navigation mechanism from the form input entirely, though it adds complexity to debugging since the data is hidden inside the server-side session store rather than being transparent in the request lifecycle.
